WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.975 --> 00:00:11.624
- [Narrator] What those
characters are speaking

00:00:11.624 --> 00:00:12.941
isn't gibberish.

00:00:12.941 --> 00:00:16.035
It's actually a language called Dothraki,

00:00:16.035 --> 00:00:19.854
and it was invented by
this guy, David Peterson,

00:00:19.854 --> 00:00:22.854
in lovely Orange County, California.

00:00:23.864 --> 00:00:26.104
David Peterson is what
we call a conlanger,

00:00:26.104 --> 00:00:28.187
an inventor of languages.

00:00:29.064 --> 00:00:33.183
- Conlanging is the intentional
creation of a full language,

00:00:33.183 --> 00:00:36.804
creating its full grammar,
its full phonology,

00:00:36.804 --> 00:00:38.744
its writing system if it has one,

00:00:38.744 --> 00:00:40.661
and its entire lexicon.

00:00:41.576 --> 00:00:43.144
- [Narrator] David has
written many languages,

00:00:43.144 --> 00:00:46.488
but the first one he
wrote was back in 2000.

00:00:46.488 --> 00:00:48.457
- [David] I was taking an
intro linguistics class

00:00:48.457 --> 00:00:50.422
at UC Berkeley in 2000,

00:00:50.422 --> 00:00:52.547
and it was just one day during class

00:00:52.547 --> 00:00:56.262
I started scribbling on my
notes my very first language.

00:00:56.262 --> 00:00:58.043
- [Narrator] He named
it after his girlfriend.

00:00:58.043 --> 00:00:58.964
- Meg Davy.

00:00:58.964 --> 00:01:00.737
- [Narrator] And according
to David himself.

00:01:00.737 --> 00:01:02.385
- It's irredeemably awful,

00:01:02.385 --> 00:01:06.135
it's just not worth even
considering anymore.

00:01:07.747 --> 00:01:09.148
Everything I was doing with this language

00:01:09.148 --> 00:01:12.088
was just kind of a
really fancy bizarre way

00:01:12.088 --> 00:01:13.601
to speak English.

00:01:13.601 --> 00:01:15.992
So I just kind of started
over and said, “Alright,

00:01:15.992 --> 00:01:20.299
ground zero, how do I
be a language creator?”

00:01:20.299 --> 00:01:21.709
- [Narrator] Before we move forward,

00:01:21.709 --> 00:01:23.563
let's take a quick step
back and talk about

00:01:23.563 --> 00:01:25.827
the godfather of modern conlanging:

00:01:25.827 --> 00:01:27.587
J.R.R. Tolkien.

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:30.049
You probably know this name
because he's the author

00:01:30.049 --> 00:01:32.847
of The Hobbit and Lord of the Rings.

00:01:32.847 --> 00:01:34.669
He created 14 different languages

00:01:34.669 --> 00:01:36.610
for use within Middle Earth.

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:39.731
- Quenya and Sindarin are
probably his most famous.

00:01:39.731 --> 00:01:42.109
He was the very first
person that we know of

00:01:42.109 --> 00:01:44.152
to create languages just for fun,

00:01:44.152 --> 00:01:47.584
and we've been kind of
emulating him ever since.

00:01:47.584 --> 00:01:48.707
(knocking)

00:01:48.707 --> 00:01:49.565
- [Narrator] Now that
we've cleared that up

00:01:49.565 --> 00:01:50.787
we can move forward.

00:01:50.787 --> 00:01:52.184
(doorbell rings)

00:01:52.184 --> 00:01:53.768
We're here in California
because we're curious

00:01:53.768 --> 00:01:57.418
about what it takes to
create a successful language.

00:01:57.418 --> 00:01:59.722
David's having two of his
friends who are also conlangers

00:01:59.722 --> 00:02:01.582
come over and speak with us.

00:02:01.582 --> 00:02:03.124
One of them brought ice cream.

00:02:03.124 --> 00:02:05.267
We were gonna do this
outside but it's too hot

00:02:05.267 --> 00:02:07.975
so we're gonna do it inside.

00:02:07.975 --> 00:02:09.142
The three of them are talking about

00:02:09.142 --> 00:02:10.717
how languages are created,

00:02:10.717 --> 00:02:13.805
and they've come up with five steps.

00:02:13.805 --> 00:02:14.845
Take it away guys.

00:02:14.845 --> 00:02:15.880
- Oh.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:17.581
So, alright.

00:02:17.581 --> 00:02:20.701
- [David] A naturalistic
conlang needs five basic things.

00:02:20.701 --> 00:02:21.534
- One.

00:02:22.689 --> 00:02:23.733
The people.

00:02:23.733 --> 00:02:25.872
- What they do, who their neighbors are.

00:02:25.872 --> 00:02:27.173
- The second thing that
you're going to need

00:02:27.173 --> 00:02:29.072
is a phonology or sound system.

00:02:29.072 --> 00:02:31.755
- Do you have voiced and
voiceless consonants?

00:02:31.755 --> 00:02:33.616
Do you have aspirated consonants?

00:02:33.616 --> 00:02:34.866
- Three, words.

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:38.533
- All the nouns, all the
verbs, all the modifiers.

00:02:38.533 --> 00:02:40.575
- Fourth, grammar.

00:02:40.575 --> 00:02:42.432
- Which is the fun part.

00:02:42.432 --> 00:02:44.015
- Finally, history.

00:02:45.091 --> 00:02:47.936
- You need to know how meanings
of words changed over time

00:02:47.936 --> 00:02:50.496
and you need to know how
the grammar of your language

00:02:50.496 --> 00:02:52.832
evolved from a time in the distant past

00:02:52.832 --> 00:02:54.533
to what's used now.

00:02:54.533 --> 00:02:55.755
- [Narrator] Now that
we've got that settled,

00:02:55.755 --> 00:02:57.013
let's finish this.

00:02:57.013 --> 00:02:59.493
- In the past 15 years,
somebody who created language

00:02:59.493 --> 00:03:02.748
went from somebody who's
like, oh, you're a nerd,

00:03:02.748 --> 00:03:04.449
to, you know, oh wow.

00:03:04.449 --> 00:03:08.307
That's really nerdy, which now means cool.

00:03:08.307 --> 00:03:10.512
- [Narrator] And its prolific
use in television and film

00:03:10.512 --> 00:03:13.468
proves that it's not just a passing fancy.

00:03:13.468 --> 00:03:16.326
(speaking foreign language)

00:03:16.326 --> 00:03:17.877
- For television the difference between

00:03:17.877 --> 00:03:20.576
a created language and some other set prop

00:03:20.576 --> 00:03:22.773
is that the maximum level of interaction

00:03:22.773 --> 00:03:25.893
that a viewer ever has with a
background set or with a prop

00:03:25.893 --> 00:03:27.557
is just seeing it on screen.

00:03:27.557 --> 00:03:29.077
A language, on the other hand,

00:03:29.077 --> 00:03:33.795
when you hear it that's the
most real a language ever gets.

00:03:33.795 --> 00:03:36.736
For that reason you have to
have a fully created language

00:03:36.736 --> 00:03:38.155
for a television show,

00:03:38.155 --> 00:03:41.413
and not just a bunch of gibberish.

00:03:41.413 --> 00:03:42.315
Was that good?

00:03:42.315 --> 00:03:44.732
- That was great.
- Thank god.

